(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-easy-2026-001"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-easy-2026-001","title":"Advance Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering","seriesId":"cs-easy-2026","num":1,"category":"advance","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Computer_Science_Engineering/002_Advance Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a certain code language, if the word DISTANCE is coded as EDCINSAT then ACQUIRE is coded as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"EACIQUR"},{"key":"B","html":"EACRIUQ"},{"key":"C","html":"ERCIAQV"},{"key":"D","html":"EARCIQU"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5f/5fe9736615c288b82d2c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><em>ABCDEF</em> is a regular hexagon of side 6 cm. What is the area of triangle <em>BDF</em>?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6a22ffd774219aa27a5b.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b4fa9c27cc2c1caa850b.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c08c66c1f19cbc67c8b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"24 cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dc7eb4147dc1f339f726.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If log<sub>3</sub> 2, log<sub>3</sub>(2 <sup>x</sup> – 5) and log<sub>3</sub> (2 <sup>x</sup> – 7/2) are in <em>AP</em>, then <em>x</em> is equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"3"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"either of (a) or (b)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8a/8ae6cdc1a7d189aa3978.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In a group of 132 people; 50, 60, 70 people like different sweets – Barfi, Jalebi, Rasgulla respectively. The number of people who like all the three sweets is half the number of people who like exactly 2 sweets. The number of people who like Barfi and Jalebi only, Jalebi and Rasgulla only and Barfi and Rasgulla only is same. The number of people who like all the three sweets are ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>12 (12 - 12)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea8c54977dece4b4087e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p><em>x</em>, 17, 3<em>x</em> – y – 2 and 3<em>x</em> + y – 30 are 4 consecutive terms of an arithmetic progression. The value of <em>x</em> + y is ________ .</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[29,29],"answerText":"29","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>29 (29 - 29)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91658d8291ce4606b18e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If a , <em>a<sub>2</sub></em> , <em>a <sub>3</sub></em> ........... <em>a<sub>n</sub></em> are in <em>AP</em>, where a <sub>i</sub> > 0 for all <em>i</em>, then the value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/397aacdd92a34268a90c.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/86/8626bf209cdb4cf101d6.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2ad7019b59739919bbbc.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23bf1db9fdfdec840ddb.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c7dbfe8b9c055379c16a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Karan and Arjun run a 100 m race, where Karan beats Arjun by 10 m. To do a favour to Arjun, Karan starts 10 m behind the starting line in a second 100 m race. They both run at earlier speeds.<br />\nWhich of the following is true in connection with the second race?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Karan and Arjun reach the finishing line simultaneously."},{"key":"B","html":"Arjun beats Karan by 1 m"},{"key":"C","html":"Arjun beats Karan by 1.1 m"},{"key":"D","html":"Karan beats Arjun by 1 m"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c68c7ab35d1bbe567389.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Select the correct mirror image of the given figure when the mirror is placed to the right side of the figure.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b948fd6289623b9672d.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7dc04f6635f76ca5741d.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3eab4e6dc7d14c9e0663.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a1/a1036a95a96007c16fd2.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7c/7c9356741e21398db381.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Choose the pair of words which have a relationship between themselves which is similar to the relationship between the given pair:<br />\nSQUANDER : MONEY ::</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"trespass : property"},{"key":"B","html":"dally : time"},{"key":"C","html":"procrastinate : task"},{"key":"D","html":"accomplish : success"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n(CAUSE AND EFFECT) When one squanders, one wastes money, when one dallies, one wastes time.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In film and videotape, it is possible to induce viewers to project their feelings onto characters on the screen. In one study, when a camera shot of a woman’s face was preceded by a shot of a baby in a crib, the audience thought the woman’s face was registering happiness. When the same shot of the woman’s face was preceded by a shot of a lion running toward the camera, the audience thought the woman’s face was registering fear. Television news teams must be careful to avoid such manipulation of their viewers.<br />\nWhich of the following can be inferred from the passage?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Television news teams have abused their position of trust in the past"},{"key":"B","html":"The expression on the woman’s face was, in actuality, blank."},{"key":"C","html":"A camera shot of a baby in a crib provoked feelings of happiness in the audience."},{"key":"D","html":"Audiences should strive to be less gullible."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThis is an inference question. The test writers are probably not interested in the conclusion of the passage. Considering the given options, we&rsquo;ll look for a statement that seems so obvious that it almost doesn&rsquo;t need saying.<br />\n&bull; Television news teams have abused their position of trust in the past. If you chose this answer,<br />\nyou inferred too much. The passage doesn&rsquo;t say that news teams have ever abused their position<br />\nof trust. Eliminate it.<br />\n&bull; The expression on the woman&rsquo;s face was, in actuality, blank. The audience had no idea what<br />\nthe expression on the woman&rsquo;s face was, and neither do we.<br />\n&bull; A camera shot of a baby in a crib provoked feelings of happiness in the audience. This is the<br />\nbest answer. The passage says that the audience projects its own feelings onto characters on<br />\nthe screen. If the audience believes the woman&rsquo;s face reflects happiness, then that must have<br />\nbeen its own reaction.<br />\n&bull; Audiences should strive to be less gullible. Rather it suggests Television news teams to be<br />\ncareful to avoid manipulation of their viewers. This statement goes way beyond the intent of the passage.<br />\nHence, option (c) is correct.</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The base (<em>x</em>) of the number system for which the below equation holds good is ________.<br />\n(516)<sub>10</sub> = (1004)<sub>x</sub></p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8 (8 - 8)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9dd8644a52c321afa460.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following languages <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> on Σ = (<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>) :</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18843eba7799f5cf78f2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d10ce5ad82812165c4c.png\" /> then what is the language <em>L</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bc43ac1ecb1749bd5c87.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/caf73857af961c23ab37.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6ba1b4369cc32b438b6.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/63adc6741ea5e1008ce7.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/47/47989aaa95a60a13a6e9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>P</em>(<em>x</em>) and <em>Q</em>(<em>x</em>) be predicates defined over a domain <em>D</em>.<br />\nConsider the universal conditional statement:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/71112f14576a583e9e0a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which ONE of the following conditions guarantees that the above universal implication is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f705c4746f4189b692b1.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d802a3bc4a1634a2ed07.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e35b71bc6b3cdd4f0e06.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/79eae04bd08ac86fb08b.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cb8061f2c59447025ea7.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":14,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the Knapsack instance below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6a08216415786b748c2e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Knapsack problem is solved using maximum, profit per unit weight. How much partial weight is taken from any object? ________</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c4/c48eec99da2025b1f793.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A sender uses stop and wait ARQ to send data.<br />\nFrame size = 2000 bits<br />\nPropagation delay = 15 millisec<br />\nBandwidth = 200 Kbps<br />\nPercentage of link utilization of stop and wait ARQ is ________?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"15"},{"key":"B","html":"35"},{"key":"C","html":"25"},{"key":"D","html":"20"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/6686c2be06d22ca2cb9c.png\" /></p>"},{"n":16,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a disk has 200 cylinders, numbered from 0 to 199. At some time the disk arm is at cylinder 100, and moving towards right direction. There is a queue of disk access requests for cylinders 30, 85, 110, 100, 105, 126, 135, 55 and 195. What will be the distance traversed by the R/W head when CSCAN algorithm is used ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[383,383],"answerText":"383","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>383</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7b20f9ef40cfcc13b660.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a hypothetical 60 bit CPU which supports 1 word instruction placed in 32 MB memory space. Instruction designed with 2 register operand, one memory operand and immediate constant fields. Instruction set size is 500. Register file size is 256. Range of a signed constants possible in the instruction is ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"{–256 to +255}"},{"key":"B","html":"{0 to 511}"},{"key":"C","html":"{–1024 to +1023}"},{"key":"D","html":"{–512 to +511}"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cc509d57c00bda2e5d91.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b0/b06a235d52d93b77b296.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Where T is non-terminal and +, ( , ), *, t are terminals.<br />\nFirst(T) and Follow(T) be denoted by set P and set R respectively. Find the P and R</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4dda7f846f9f7fba1854.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/97/97caf2615b58d24a5fbe.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3a2f1b17d63ee5126c33.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/76/762cc865d54d3286d697.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/39de8c82e2615ef0d77f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":19,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statement:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/872e128a60acb95aa134.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) = Ο(<em>h</em>(<em>n</em>))"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) = Ο( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>))"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) + <em>h</em>(<em>n</em>) = Ο(<em>g</em>(<em>n</em>) + <em>h</em>(<em>n</em>))"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8e/8ea97d7071286a51351b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>R</em> = ABCDE and functional dependencies:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c3/c3cf21dcf5e5aa819b07.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Consider the decomposition: <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> (A, B, C) and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>(B, C, D, E).<br />\nWhich statement is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The decomposition is dependency-preserving but not lossless."},{"key":"B","html":"The decomposition is lossless but not dependency-preserving."},{"key":"C","html":"The decomposition is both dependency-preserving and lossless."},{"key":"D","html":"The decomposition is neither dependency-preserving nor lossless."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/136e9125d2803634c291.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of following is true about the IPv4 NOP (No operation) option?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It signals the end of all IP options in header."},{"key":"B","html":"It is used to align subsequent IP options on 32 bit boundaries."},{"key":"C","html":"It forces routers to record timestamps."},{"key":"D","html":"It is used for security authenticated in IP packets."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nNOP is used to fill the gaps between option (b) is answer.</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following regular expression (RE)<br />\nRE = (aa + ab)<sup>+</sup> (a + b)<sup>+</sup> (a + b)<sup>*</sup><br />\nHow many minimal strings exist for the above RE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3e1671a4483854b1faf7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is a proper state equation for the circuit given below?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b2ec371e60c6d9bc5c1.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cce04f48b0b6b46f030b.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f7fc3452f6b6ba195ef0.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef03f36014cf298d519b.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0f/0f467d5c0e83d3e0fc0b.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/196ee72a9a88191cbed4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which statement correctly describes the IP source routing option?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It allows routers to modify the IP address of sender."},{"key":"B","html":"It allows the sender to specify the exact route a packet must follow."},{"key":"C","html":"It prevents packets from being fragmented."},{"key":"D","html":"It is used for NAT transmission in private networks."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIn case of source routing, packets follow the path that is specified by source.</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system with five resources P, Q, R, S, T and four processes the process allocation table is given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/05/0558cf3815f19892d025.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The smallest value of X for which the above system in safe state ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bf412526b4308da3a74c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A processor has 256 register and uses 32 bit instruction format. In has two type of instructions:<br />\nI-type and R-type. Each I-type instruction contain an opcode, a register name and 12 bit immediate value. Each R type instruction contain an opcode and two register names. If there are 1024 distinct I-type opcodes, the maximum number of distinct R-type opcodes is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[49152,49152],"answerText":"49152","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>49152</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1ddc9827dcded00d5b3e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Given the 3-address code for a basic block:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3def01c2c17ea3db2d81.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>How many registers are needed to allocate this basic block with no spills?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : In Deterministic Quick Sort, an adversary can always provide an input that leads to Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>) time.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In Selection Sort, the position of the minimum element found in each pass does not depend on previous swaps, which makes its running time independent of the initial ordering of elements.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Deterministic Quick Sort chooses pivots using a fixed rule (for example, always the first element).<br />\nAn adversary can therefore feed a sorted or reverse-sorted array, ensuring each partition is<br />\nmaximally unbalanced. Time complexity becomes &Omicron;(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>). So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true.<br />\n&bull; In Selection Sort, each pass scans the entire unsorted portion to locate the minimum. The<br />\nalgorithm&rsquo;s number of comparisons does not depend on whether the array is already sorted,<br />\npartially sorted, or completely random. The &ldquo;position&rdquo; of the next minimum is found purely<br />\nby comparison, unaffected by previous swaps hence the running time is independent of initial<br />\nordering. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true.</p>"},{"n":29,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> are two relations. At current instance, <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> has 17 tuples and <em>r</em><sub>2</sub> has 118 tuples. Left outer join applied on <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>r</em><sub>2</sub> with <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> on left side. Let, <em>n</em> and m are number of minimum and maximum possible tuples return by this left outer join operation. The value of <em>n</em> + <em>m</em> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2023,2023],"answerText":"2023","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2023</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/00/006a2a34ca0e2befcbad.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A pair of unbiased dice is rolled together till a sum of either 5 or 7 is obtained. The probability that 5 comes before 7 is obtained. The probability that 5 comes before 7 is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/10a9a263ad11bec15ad0.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af100b33627c14b6990f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f0c4d371c441a8d1db6.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a1/a1800c38e872191ff6dd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the combinational circuit using full adder shown below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ec/ec50a0dc0428b1801f32.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>where, <em>S</em> and <em>C</em> are sum and carry of full adder.<br />\nThe logic expression for <em>S</em> and <em>C</em> respectively are,</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/77594c51313f1123b20f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em> = 1; <em>C</em> = 0"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>S</em> = 0; <em>C</em> = 1"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3f/3fedfba7aef6c2e5eb78.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bfcfa4cc53306998649c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A LAN uses CSMA/CD with a bandwidth of 10 Mbps. The maximum one way propagation delay between two farthest stations is 256 μsec.<br />\nWhat is the minimum frame size in bytes the sender must transmit so that any collision occurring any where on the network will be detected while the sender is still transmitting ________?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"720"},{"key":"B","html":"840"},{"key":"C","html":"580"},{"key":"D","html":"640"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/814501b9ce512dbb436e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Kernel level thread uses a one to one mapping with Kernel thread.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : User level thread use a many to one mapping with Kernel thread.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Page fault in a user level thread cause the entire process to be blocked.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Only S<sub>1</sub> and S<sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> are correct"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a hypothetical 24 bits floating point format structured as follows:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/de01b834445039dd3c76.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Calculate the final product in memory (in hexadecimal) after multiplying the two data points below:<br />\n<em>A</em> = (10.25)<sub>10</sub><br />\n<em>B</em> = (2.5)<sub>10</sub></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(31CD00)<sub>16</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"(41CD00)<sub>16</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"(41BC00)<sub>16</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"(31BC00)<sub>16</sub>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e51b70e3b5574bd77f0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>When Insertion Sort is applied to a list of n unique integers:<br />\n• The running time is Ο(n) if the list is already sorted in increasing order.<br />\n• The running time is Ο(n2) if the list is sorted in decreasing order.<br />\nNow suppose a random input list is chosen such that:<br />\n• With probability<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/233dc0f50ad066e86732.png\" /> the list happens to be sorted in decreasing order.<br />\n• With probability <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/0b/0b311a42e33254b328fb.png\" /> the list is already sorted in increasing order.<br />\nFind the expected asymptotic cost (average time complexity) of Insertion Sort for this random input.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b1bf29bebfd79685ea8a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/82c8a02c3e9b40e8547d.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fccb3b7ff8293b7907a3.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35a615b04de96b45cb3a.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/df/df92a1406ea6ec570c58.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following code:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/75/759e86f874d61fdbb4dc.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The value returned by the function “find” when a pointer to the root of a non-empty tree is passed as argument is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The number of levels in the tree"},{"key":"B","html":"The number of nodes in the tree"},{"key":"C","html":"The number of internal nodes in the tree"},{"key":"D","html":"The height of the tree"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nThe given program recursively find the number of levels in left sub tree and number of levels in<br />\nright sub tree of root node, then at last it will add 1 + max (levels in left sub tree, levels in right sub tree).<br />\nWhich will result number of levels in given tree.</p>"},{"n":37,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Minimum number of levels of B<sup>+</sup> tree index required for 6000 keys and order of B<sup>+</sup> tree node (P) is 10. {P : max pointer possible to store in B<sup>+</sup> tree node} ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74795a3f3017c44e91d6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/caafbe8d8dda312789cd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[24,24],"answerText":"24","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>24</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/caafbe8d8dda312789cd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider 4 stages pipelines with a respective delays of 3 ns, 5 ns, 7 ns and 3 ns used to execute the program segment which contain 40 load instructions, 70 ALU instruction and 50 branch instructions. All instruction are spending 1 cycle on all stages (IF, ID, EX, WB). Load and ALU instruction output is available in the 3rd stages and branch instruction output is available in 4th stage. Operand forwarding is used in the pipeline. What is the program execution time in ________ ns. (When it is executed in the pipeline without branch prediction)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2425"},{"key":"B","html":"2170"},{"key":"C","html":"2191"},{"key":"D","html":"2270"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5f/5fd19f91b2ed5e9045d1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let G = (V, E) be a directed weighted graph with n vertices. The Bellman-Ford algorithm is<br />\nexecuted from source s. For a vertex <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e99952e33b029ee0b876.png\" /> denote the distance estimate after i complete iterations of edge relaxations.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : If no edge can be relaxed in the <em>n</em><sup>th</sup> iteration (i.e. after performing the usual <em>n</em> – 1 iterations we<br />\ncheck the n<sup>th</sup> and find no relaxation possible), then G has no negative-weight cycle reachable from s.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If there exists a vertex <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a8e7b6fc82c96204d47.png\" /> must lie on a negative-weight cycle.<br />\nWhich one is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are true"},{"key":"D","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are false"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Identify the language generated by following grammar where S is start variable:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/83/83b5b7e36b6882f16927.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/28/28b5dc003f776b62b0cf.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/86/8615c6cf568740e30e18.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/44/44ad58a804439d906f33.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following C program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3af364808eaed985caef.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is correct for above program?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Output of the program is 41."},{"key":"B","html":"Output of program is 11."},{"key":"C","html":"Memory allocated to variable i is in activation record of function ‘fun’."},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following graphical representation of relation:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/90/90a75c294ea471405b15.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the above graph represent partial ordered relation?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only (i)"},{"key":"B","html":"Only (ii)"},{"key":"C","html":"Both (i) and (ii)"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n<strong>In (i) graph:</strong><br />\n&bull; Self loop present on every vertex. So reflexive.<br />\n&bull; Since all arrows except self loop are one way. So antisymmetric.<br />\n&bull; Since (<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>) and (<em>b</em>, <em>d</em>) present but (<em>a</em>, <em>d</em>) edge not present. So, not transitive relation. Hence not<br />\npartial order relation.<br />\n<strong>In (ii) graph:</strong><br />\n&bull; Self loop present for every vertex. So reflexive.<br />\n&bull; Since all arrows except self loop are one way, so it is antisymmetric.<br />\n&bull; Since (<em>c</em>, <em>a</em>) and (<em>a</em>, <em>d</em>) edge present but (<em>c</em>, <em>d</em>) edge not present. So, not transitive relation.<br />\nHence not partial order relation.</p>"},{"n":44,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>B</em> is an invertible square matrix and <em>B</em> is the adjoint of matrix <em>A</em> such that <em>AB</em> = <em>B<sup>T</sup></em>. Then</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>A</em> is an identity matrix"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>B</em> is a symmetric matrix"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>A</em> is a skew-symmetric matrix"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>B</em> is a skew-symmetric matrix"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/61/61fd0be32db500aeb077.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which DNS record specifies the authoritative name server for a domain?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A"},{"key":"B","html":"NS"},{"key":"C","html":"MX"},{"key":"D","html":"CNAME"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following set of processes that need to be schedule on a single CPU. All the times are given in msec.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/15dffac5555bcd7d161b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statements is/are correct<strong>?<br />\nNote:</strong> For Round Robin [<em>T</em>.<em>Q</em>. = 3]</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Average waiting time Round Robin algorithm is greater than SRTF."},{"key":"B","html":"Average waiting time SRTF and FCFS are equal."},{"key":"C","html":"Average waiting time Round Robin and FCFS are equal."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/136bf9d7d49c26f3bc51.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6a63aca4de866d456f22.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Isolated to uses common bus for I/O and memory but different control signal."},{"key":"B","html":"Memory mapped I/O mode uses separate bus for I/O and memory."},{"key":"C","html":"Isolated I/O mode uses common bus with common control signal for both I/O and memory unit."},{"key":"D","html":"Memory mapped I/O use common bus and common control signal for both memory and I/O."}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)<br />\n&bull; Input output processor mode uses separate bus for memory and I/O unit.<br />\n&bull; Memory mapped I/O mode uses common bus with common control signal for both I/O and<br />\nmemory unit.<br />\n&bull; Isolated I/O mode uses common bus but different control signal for both I/O and memory<br />\nunit.</p>"},{"n":48,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following directed graph:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b663795be88b6b3ca248.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Suppose a depth first traversal of this graph is performed, assuming that whenever there is a choice, the vertex earlier in the alphabetical order is to be chosen. Suppose the number of tree edges is <em>T</em>, the number of back edges is <em>B</em> and the number of cross edges is <em>C</em>. Then, what is <em>B</em> + <em>C</em> + <em>T</em>?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>6</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/86/86b5fd7bdbb7492d9f37.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following code:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0d64a9059d650b126c5e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The number of token in above code are _______.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[34,34],"answerText":"34","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>34</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e26c917f125bf82e9fc.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":50,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a sequence {<em>a<sub>k</sub></em>} whose generating function is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/17/175378fefa414e97b564.png\" /></p>\n\n<p><em>a</em><sub>0</sub> and <em>a</em><sub>7</sub> are 1st and 8<sup>th</sup> term of given sequence respectively. The value of <em>a</em><sub>0</sub> + <em>a</em><sub>7</sub> = ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/5034b63889e1590450ee.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>f</em> : [–1, 1] → <em>R</em>, where <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 2<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – <em>x</em><sup>4</sup> – 10. The minimum value of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) is _______.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-13,-13],"answerText":"-13","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>-13</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53d8c3cb76b88137d5a0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Progress is NOT satisfied if there is a deadlock in code."},{"key":"B","html":"Progress is always satisfied if there is NO deadlock in code."},{"key":"C","html":"Spinlock is used for busy-wait mutual exclusion."},{"key":"D","html":"Semaphore can be used to avoid spinlock."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)</p>"},{"n":53,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following C Programming:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/3929e25dad755d99fc5c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What will be the output of the above code?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[42,42],"answerText":"42","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>42</p>"},{"n":54,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a database system that uses a basic timestamp-based concurrency control protocol. Let <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>T</em><sub>2</sub>, …, <em>T<sub>n</sub></em> be concurrent transactions with distinct timestamps.<br />\nConsider the following statements:<br />\nI. The protocol cannot enter deadlock, even if multiple transactions simultaneously request conflicting operations.<br />\nII. A transaction with an older timestamp may never complete if it repeatedly conflicts with younger transactions.<br />\nIII. In the protocol, a read operation is rejected if a younger transaction has already written the<br />\nsame data item.<br />\nIV. The protocol guarantees strict first-come-first-serve order of transaction execution.<br />\nHow many number of statements are correct?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3<br />\n&bull; Deadlocks occur when transactions wait in a cycle for resources (as in 2PL). In timestamp<br />\nprotocol, transactions that violate timestamp ordering are aborted immediately, not made to<br />\nwait. Hence deadlock is impossible.<br />\n&bull; Older transactions may keep getting aborted if younger transactions consistently conflict<br />\nwith their operations. This is called starvation.<br />\n&bull; If a younger transaction wrote a data item, an older transaction trying to read it violates<br />\ntimestamp order. The older transaction is aborted or restarted, so the read is rejected.<br />\n&bull; Execution depends on timestamps, not actual arrival order. A transaction that arrives first<br />\nmay have a later timestamp than another, so first-come-first-serve is not guaranteed.</p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the combinational circuit shown below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab1e3d4bfce1a700ceb8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>With <em>W</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>W</em><sub>2</sub> as inputs, the given circuit implements a</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"EX-OR logic"},{"key":"B","html":"EX-NOR logic"},{"key":"C","html":"OR-logic"},{"key":"D","html":"NOR logic"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/8737b72b37aafd6e4abf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>How many of the following statements about finite languages over some finite alphabet Σ are true.<br />\n<strong>A.</strong> It could be undecidable.<br />\n<strong>B.</strong> It is Turing Machine recognizable.<br />\n<strong>C.</strong> It is constant sensitive language.<br />\n<strong>D.</strong> Every finite subset of a regular language is regular.<br />\n<strong>E.</strong> Every finite subset of a non-regular language is regular.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4<br />\nEvery finite set is regular and hence also a CSL and Turing recognizable.<br />\nHence, B, C, D, E are correct.</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following situations is most likely to cause instability in a distributed routing algorithm even when all routers periodically exchange updates?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Routers use triggered updates only when significant metric change occurs."},{"key":"B","html":"Link costs fluctuate rapidly but large hold-down timer is configured."},{"key":"C","html":"Routers run the algorithm asynchronously with inconsistent views of the network."},{"key":"D","html":"Split horizon with poison reverse is applied across all interfaces."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nWhen routers compute routes based on incomplete or inconsistent topology information. In<br />\nasynchronous distributed routing, routers update at different times, causing temporary mismatches<br />\nin their routing tables. This leads to route oscillation, loops, and slow convergence, making the<br />\nalgorithm unstable.</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements regarding LR(0) parser is FALSE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A LR(0) configurating set cannot have multiple reduce items."},{"key":"B","html":"A LR(0) configurating set cannot have both shift as well as reduce items."},{"key":"C","html":"If a reduce item is present in a LR(0) configurating set it may have other item."},{"key":"D","html":"A LR(0) parser can parse any regular grammar."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nNo grammar with empty production can be LR(0). But empty rules are allowed in regular grammar<br />\nas the only condition for a grammar to be regular is to be either left linear or right linear.</p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> be two 4-bit registers that store numbers in 1’s complement form. For the operation<br />\nR<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>, which one of the following values of R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> gives an arithmetic overflow?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1011 and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = 1110"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1100 and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = 1010"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1111 and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = 1000"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1001 and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = 1111"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d0/d093e5e60d8984e40a5e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A stack is implemented using a circular doubly linked list such that PUSH and POP operations are performed efficiently. Which one of the following statements can be correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both operations can be performed in Ο(1) time."},{"key":"B","html":"The worst case time complexity for both operations will be Ω(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"C","html":"Stack can not be implemented with the given data structure."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c8/c8a47326f4bf8e8cc4ee.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e73a817b9e5989d326e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A computer system’s page fault rate decreases as the amount of main memory increases. Every time the memory is doubled, the page fault rate becomes one fourth of its previous value. Currently, the system has 16 MB of memory, and the page fault rate is 4%. The main memory access time is 600 ns and overall effective memory access time is 250 μs. If the memory is expanded to 64 MB,<br />\ncalculate the new EMAT is microseconds ________? (Upto 2 decimal)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[16,16.4],"answerText":"16.18","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>16.18 [16.00 - 16.40]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d43018b136e0f499ee65.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/1556c262d1bca136468b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is correct regarding the entry in the predictive parsing table (LL(1) parsing table) M for above grammar?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"M[T, (] = T → FT′, M[T′, +] = T′ → ∈"},{"key":"B","html":"M[E′, )] = E′ → ∈, M[E′, *] → E′ → ∈"},{"key":"C","html":"M[E, (] = E → TE′, M[E, *] → E → ∈"},{"key":"D","html":"M[T, id] = T → FT′, M[T, )] = T → FT′"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d16b25988fc1604eb2cf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":63,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following matrices with their dimensions:<br />\nA → 15 × 20<br />\nB → 20 × 10<br />\nC → 10 × 25<br />\nD → 25 × 15<br />\nE → 15 × 500<br />\nApply matrix chain multiplication algorithm to find the minimum number of operations required.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[121500,121500],"answerText":"121500","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>121500</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/1934deb4c4d79bb1902b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":64,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The eigen values of the following matrix are represented by λ<sub>1</sub> and λ<sub>2</sub></p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e958bd7ae9c95a1d884.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0d5ef6f5c65f6fce9799.png\" /> is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.3,0.35],"answerText":"0.33","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.33 [0.30 - 0.35]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/918295d02444ddd7c3e9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A direct-mapped cache size 4 KB is organized into 1024 bytes blocks.<br />\nThe following main memory blocks are referred to by the CPU during program execution:<br />\n5, 9, 8, 3, 2, 6, 2, 5, 7, 6, 5, 9, 6, 9<br />\nWhich of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Number of conflict misses are 4."},{"key":"B","html":"Number of compulsory misses are 7."},{"key":"C","html":"Hit ratio of a program is 0.21."},{"key":"D","html":"Hit ratio of a program is 0.42."}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/ccde52a4ee83cf61778c.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":38,"NAT":21,"MSQ":6},"withoutKey":0}};
